D={
"Classical Golden Age of India (Gupta)":("From about 320 to 550 CE, the Gupta Empire under Chandragupta I, Samudragupta and Chandragupta II ruled north India from Pataliputra and Ujjain. It was a classical age of Sanskrit literature (Kalidasa's Shakuntala and Meghaduta), mathematics and astronomy (Aryabhata's place-value system and rotating Earth), the codification of Puranas and law books, early stone temples such as Deogarh, and the paintings of Ajanta under the allied Vakatakas. Nalanda was founded around 427. Huna invasions and the rise of feudatories ended Gupta power by the mid-6th century.","The Chinese pilgrim Faxian (about 405) described prosperous towns, free hospitals, mild punishments and many vegetarians. Guilds of merchants and artisans were powerful, gold coins circulated, and kings increasingly granted land to Brahmins and temples."),
"Late Antique Little Ice Age":("Three major volcanic eruptions in 536, 540 and 547 CE cooled the Northern Hemisphere by up to 2 °C for over a century (until about 660), according to tree rings. Crop failures, the Plague of Justinian from 541 and migrations of steppe peoples like the Avars coincided. The Byzantine and Sasanian empires were weakened just before the Arab conquests, and the Chinese Sui and Tang dynasties rose from the turmoil.","Farmers faced short, cold summers and famine; plague emptied cities; some regions saw people move to new lands."),
"Early Middle Ages":("From about 500 to 1000 CE, the Western Roman Empire gave way to Germanic kingdoms — Franks, Visigoths, Anglo-Saxons — while the Byzantine Empire continued in the east. Islam rose and spread from Spain to Sindh; Tang China flourished; in India, the Chalukyas, Pallavas, Rashtrakutas, Palas and Pratiharas competed, and temple architecture blossomed. The Vikings raided Europe from 793, and Charlemagne briefly revived a western empire.","In Europe most people were peasants tied to lords' estates under feudalism, while monasteries preserved learning; in India, village assemblies and temples organised local life."),
"Islamic Golden Age":("From roughly the 8th to the 13th century, scholars in Baghdad's House of Wisdom, Cairo, Córdoba, Bukhara and Samarkand translated and expanded Greek, Persian and Indian knowledge. Al-Khwarizmi founded algebra and spread Indian numerals; Ibn al-Haytham pioneered optics and experimental method; Ibn Sina wrote the Canon of Medicine; al-Biruni studied India; astronomers built observatories. Paper, learned from China, made books cheap. The Mongol sack of Baghdad in 1258 is often seen as its end.","Cities had hospitals (bimaristans), libraries, public baths and markets; merchants traded from Spain to China, and Muslims, Christians and Jews worked together in scholarship."),
"Medieval Warm Period":("From about 950 to 1250, the North Atlantic and parts of the world were warmer. Vikings settled Iceland and Greenland and reached North America; Europe's population roughly doubled; vineyards grew in England. Elsewhere it brought droughts, contributing to the decline of the Ancestral Puebloans and possibly Angkor's water stress later.","Longer growing seasons let European peasants clear new land; towns, cathedrals and trade grew."),
"Chola and medieval India":("From the 9th to the 13th centuries, the Cholas ruled south India and Sri Lanka and raided Southeast Asia, building the Brihadeeswarar temple. The Palas in Bengal patronised Buddhist universities, Rajputs ruled the north, and temple building reached its peak at Khajuraho, Konark and Bhubaneswar. Mahmud of Ghazni's raids and the Ghurid conquest led to the Delhi Sultanate in 1206.","Temple towns organised irrigation, schools, banking and festivals; village assemblies like Uttaramerur's elected committees by lot; and merchant guilds traded overseas."),
"Mongol era":("In the 13th and 14th centuries the Mongol Empire, founded by Genghis Khan, became the largest contiguous land empire, stretching from Korea to Hungary. Conquests killed millions, but the Pax Mongolica revived the Silk Road, allowing Marco Polo and Ibn Battuta to travel, and spreading gunpowder, printing and paper money west. The Delhi Sultanate repelled Mongol invasions. It split into the Yuan, Ilkhanate, Chagatai and Golden Horde.","Steppe nomads lived in felt gers, herding horses and sheep; conquered city-dwellers paid taxes and supplied craftsmen, while merchants enjoyed safe roads."),
"Black Death era":("The Black Death, caused by Yersinia pestis, reached Crimea in 1346 and swept through the Middle East, North Africa and Europe by 1353, killing about a third to half of Europe's people and many elsewhere. Labour shortages raised wages and weakened serfdom; peasants rebelled (England 1381); Jews were persecuted; art became preoccupied with death. Plague recurred for centuries.","Survivors found land plentiful and labour scarce, improving the lot of peasants and workers in Western Europe."),
"Little Ice Age":("From about 1300 to 1850, global temperatures fell slightly, with colder periods in the 1600s linked to low solar activity and volcanic eruptions. Glaciers advanced in the Alps, the Thames froze for 'frost fairs', and crop failures contributed to famines and revolts in the 17th-century 'General Crisis' — including the fall of the Ming and the Thirty Years' War. India and China suffered droughts and famines.","Harsh winters, failed harvests and higher food prices made life precarious; people adapted with new crops like the potato."),
"Delhi Sultanate and Vijayanagara":("From 1206 to 1526, the Delhi Sultanate's Mamluk, Khalji, Tughlaq, Sayyid and Lodi dynasties ruled north India, repelling the Mongols. In the south, Vijayanagara (1336–1646) and the Bahmani Sultanate competed. Indo-Islamic architecture, the Persian wheel, Urdu–Hindavi, and the Bhakti and Sufi movements developed.","Peasants paid land revenue to iqtadars and nayakas; towns grew around forts and markets; saints like Kabir, Nizamuddin Auliya and Chaitanya preached devotion."),
"Renaissance":("Beginning in 14th-century Florence and spreading across Europe by the 16th, the Renaissance revived classical Greek and Roman learning. Greek scholars fleeing Constantinople, the printing press and wealthy patrons like the Medici fuelled art (Leonardo, Michelangelo, Raphael), literature (Petrarch, Shakespeare), and science (Copernicus, Vesalius). Humanism focused on human potential.","Merchants and bankers grew rich; workshops trained artists; and printed books became affordable to many."),
"Age of Exploration":("From the 15th to 17th centuries, Portuguese and Spanish sailors, followed by the Dutch, English and French, used caravels, the compass and astrolabe to reach Africa, India, the Americas and the Pacific. Vasco da Gama reached Calicut (1498), Columbus the Caribbean (1492), and Magellan's crew circled the globe (1519–22). It began colonial empires, the Columbian Exchange and the Atlantic slave trade.","Sailors faced scurvy and storms; port cities from Lisbon to Goa boomed; and Indigenous peoples faced conquest and disease."),
"Mughal era":("From Babur's victory at Panipat in 1526 to the deposition of Bahadur Shah Zafar in 1857, the Mughals ruled much of South Asia, reaching their height under Akbar, Jahangir, Shah Jahan and Aurangzeb. They built the Taj Mahal, Red Fort and Fatehpur Sikri, developed the mansabdari system and produced 25% of world manufacturing output. After 1707 the empire fragmented into successor states.","Peasants grew wheat, rice, cotton and indigo and paid about a third of their produce; cities like Agra, Lahore and Delhi were among the world's largest; and artisans produced fine textiles."),
"Colonial era":("From the 16th to the mid-20th century, European powers colonised the Americas, much of Asia and nearly all of Africa. Colonial rule extracted resources and labour, imposed borders, spread European languages and Christianity, and caused famines and genocides, while also introducing railways and modern education. Independence movements ended most empires after 1945.","Colonised peoples paid heavy taxes, worked on plantations and mines, and resisted through revolts, reform and nationalism."),
"Scientific Revolution and Enlightenment":("From Copernicus (1543) to Newton's Principia (1687), observation and mathematics transformed science; the Enlightenment of the 18th century applied reason to politics and society. Thinkers like Locke, Voltaire, Rousseau and Kant argued for rights, tolerance and limits on power, inspiring the American and French Revolutions.","Scientific societies, coffee houses, newspapers and encyclopaedias spread ideas among the growing middle class."),
"Industrial Revolution":("Beginning in Britain around 1760, machines, coal and steam power transformed textiles, iron and transport, spreading to Europe, the US and Japan. Railways, factories and cities grew rapidly. Cheap British textiles undercut Indian handloom weavers, contributing to deindustrialisation. It brought great wealth but also child labour, pollution and slums.","Workers moved from farms to cities and laboured long hours in factories; trade unions and socialism arose in response."),
"British Raj":("From 1858, after the 1857 rebellion, to 1947, India was ruled by the British Crown through a Viceroy. Railways, telegraphs, canals and universities were built, but famines killed tens of millions and wealth drained to Britain. The national movement grew from the Congress (1885) to Gandhi's mass campaigns and independence with Partition.","Peasants faced high revenue and famines; an English-educated middle class grew; and reformers fought caste and gender injustices."),
"World wars era":("From 1914 to 1945, two world wars killed about 100 million people. Empires collapsed, the Russian Revolution created the Soviet Union, fascism rose in Italy and Germany, the Great Depression struck, and the Holocaust murdered six million Jews. The atomic bomb ended the second war.","Civilians faced conscription, rationing, bombing and occupation; women entered factories; and colonies supplied soldiers and resources."),
"Cold War":("From 1947 to 1991 the US and the Soviet Union competed without direct war, through an arms race, the space race and proxy wars in Korea, Vietnam and Afghanistan. Decolonisation created new nations; India led the Non-Aligned Movement. It ended with the collapse of the USSR.","People lived with the threat of nuclear war while consumer societies grew in the West and planned economies in the East."),
"Information Age":("Since the 1970s, microchips, personal computers, the internet (1983), the World Wide Web (1991) and smartphones have transformed communication, work and politics. India became a major IT services centre, and digital payments reached hundreds of millions. Artificial intelligence is the latest phase.","Most people now carry a connected device; information spreads instantly, along with misinformation."),
"Anthropocene":("Scientists proposed the Anthropocene as a new epoch defined by human impact on Earth: climate change from greenhouse gases, plastic and radioactive markers in sediments, species extinctions and altered landscapes. In 2024 geologists voted not to formally adopt it as an epoch, but the term is widely used.","Rising temperatures, heatwaves and biodiversity loss affect everyone, prompting climate action."),
}
